Eosinophilic Oesophagitis EoE
Complex Fatigue Entities
Chronic immune system disease of the esophagus

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-014
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of acute dysphagia and perceived food impaction to rule out complete airway or esophageal obstruction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported sudden onset of difficulty swallowing solid food with retrosternal chest pain and hypersalivation. Immediate in-person assessment was critical to confirm airway patency, exclude acute myocardial infarction mimicking esophageal spasm, and determine if urgent endoscopic disimpaction was necessary before regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I swallowed some chicken and it feels stuck right behind my breastbone; I can't even swallow my own spit without pain.
History of Present Illness: Patient with a known atopic history (asthma/eczema) reports an acute episode of dysphagia occurring mid-meal 2 hours ago. The patient describes a sudden sensation of food impaction in the mid-esophagus. Associated symptoms include retrosternal pressure, frequent regurgitation of thick saliva, and significant anxiety. The patient states they have had progressive, intermittent dysphagia with dry solids over the last 6 months, but tonight is the first acute impaction event.
Functional Status: Ambulatory but severely distressed, pacing and spitting into a basin.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of aspiration if vomiting occurs while supine.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 mmHg | HR: 102 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 20 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Anxious, actively expectorating saliva into a container, frequently pointing to the mid-sternum.
Physical Exam: Airway is completely patent; speaking in full sentences without stridor. Oropharynx clear. Respiratory exam reveals equal air entry bilaterally with no wheeze or signs of aspiration. Cardiovascular exam is unremarkable; normal heart sounds. Abdomen is soft and non-tender. Presentation strongly aligns with a chronic immune system disease of the esophagus causing an acute fibrotic stricture or spasm.
Environmental Observations: Uneaten meal on table. Basin full of frothy saliva.
Medication Reconciliation: Uses an over-the-counter antacid occasionally with no relief of swallowing difficulties.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Eosinophilic Oesophagitis EoE - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear mechanical trigger swallowing solid food, absence of radiating pain, shortness of breath, or diaphoresis, and localized nature of the complaint.

Complete esophageal rupture Boerhaave syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of severe shock, subcutaneous emphysema, or unremitting excruciating pleuritic chest pain.

Achalasia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of an atopic terrain and episodic nature primarily with dry solids, though definitive diagnosis requires manometry and endoscopy.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute retrosternal chest pain and dysphagia following a meal. Immediate after-hours evaluation was absolutely necessary to rule out a life-threatening acute coronary syndrome, which can present atypically, and to evaluate for complete esophageal obstruction or aspiration risk. Upon confirming airway patency and hemodynamic stability, clinical intervention was required to attempt pharmacological relaxation of the esophagus to facilitate transit of the suspected bolus, avoiding an unnecessary emergency department admission if resolution could be achieved on-site.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Diazepam 5mg and sublingual GTN (Nitroglycerin) cautiously to induce smooth muscle relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter. Patient successfully passed a small sip of water 30 minutes post-intervention, confirming resolution of complete impaction. Targeted intervention strategy includes PEA, Zinc, and Initiation of an Elimination/Wahls-based protocol.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly cutting your solid foods into pea-sized pieces and chewing each bite 20 times, you will mechanically predigest the food, directly preventing large boluses from hitting the strictures and rewarding yourself with pain-free meals.
Home-Based Services Optional: Referral to dietician for elimination diet guidance.
DME/Safety Optional: None required.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours for referral to gastroenterology for diagnostic endoscopy and biopsies.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to Emergency Department if the inability to swallow fluids returns, if severe chest pain recurs, or if vomiting of blood occurs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal endoscopic evaluation and esophageal biopsies to confirm eosinophilic infiltration.
Clinical Course: While the acute impaction has cleared, the underlying inflammatory strictures remain. Without dietary modification and targeted therapy, repeat impactions are highly likely.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if the crushing chest pain returns, if you start struggling to breathe, or if you vomit dark red blood.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your esophagus is inflamed due to an allergic-type reaction, making it rigid and prone to clamping down on food; the medications temporarily relaxed the muscle so the trapped food could pass.
Actionable Steps: Adhere strictly to a soft-liquid diet (soups, smoothies) for the next 48 hours; removing mechanical trauma allows the esophageal lining to rest, positively reinforcing immediate symptom reduction while awaiting endoscopy.

